          "type": "turn",
          "question": "If someone has a raised blood pressure reading but no diagnosis of hypertension, which chapter does that belong in?",
          "answer": "A raised blood pressure reading with no diagnosis of hypertension is classified in Chapter 18 of the ICD-10-CM system, which covers symptoms, signs and abnormal findings (codes R00 to R99). In the chapter's circulatory and respiratory section, it sits at R00 to R09. This comes from the document \"ICD-10-CM Chapter 18: symptoms, signs and abnormal findings (R00-R99).\"\n\nThe hypertension code, I10, is not used in this case. The coding guidance says a single raised reading, or a series of readings, is treated as a finding rather than a disease until a clinician records a diagnosis. The diagnosis in the record decides the code, not the number itself. This is from \"Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family.\"\n\nThe documents do not say what a raised reading means for a particular person, what tests might follow, or when to see a clinician. Those questions are worth raising with a doctor or nurse. This is a classification answer only, not guidance about any individual's health.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
